    body: `
<p>English adds -s: one ticket, two tickets. Russian changes the last letter of the noun instead, and which letter depends on the noun's gender. There is no -s anywhere. The good news: the choice is almost always between two letters, and you can see which one from the end of the noun. This lesson covers the plural in "these are", "here are" and "where are" sentences; later lessons add the endings plurals take after other words.</p>
<h3>Masculine and feminine: ы or и</h3>
<p>A masculine noun that ends in a consonant adds <b>ы</b>: <b>билет → билеты</b>. A feminine noun swaps its <b>а</b> for <b>ы</b>: <b>машина → машины</b>. Nouns that end in <b>й</b>, <b>я</b> or <b>ь</b> take <b>и</b>: <b>музей → музеи</b>, <b>кухня → кухни</b>.</p>
{{ex:Это мои билеты.|EH-tah mah-EE bee-LYEH-tih|These are my tickets.}}
<h3>The spelling rule</h3>
<p>After <b>г к х ж ш щ ч</b>, Russian never writes ы: it writes и. So <b>книга → книги</b>, <b>сумка → сумки</b>, <b>ключ → ключи</b>. Your ear helps: after к and г you hear "ee", as in English <i>key</i>.</p>
{{ex:Где мои ключи?|gdyeh mah-EE klyoo-CHEE|Where are my keys?}}
{{ex:Вот новые книги.|voht NOH-vih-yih KNEE-gee|Here are the new books.}}
<h3>Neuter: а or я</h3>
<p>Neuter nouns swap <b>о</b> for <b>а</b>, and <b>е</b> for <b>я</b>: <b>окно → окна</b>, <b>письмо → письма</b>, <b>здание → здания</b>. Watch the stress: it often jumps. <b>окно</b> is stressed at the end, but <b>окна</b> at the start. Always check the capitals in the respelling, and listen to the audio: <b>окна</b> said with the stress at the end is a different word, "of the window".</p>
{{ex:Какие большие окна!|kah-KEE-yih bahl'-SHIH-yih OHK-nah|What big windows!}}
<h3>Learn these by heart</h3>
<p>English has a few odd ones too (man, men; child, children). Russian's most common ones:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>One</th><th>More than one</th></tr><tr><td class="k">человек</td><td class="k">люди</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ребёнок</td><td class="k">дети</td></tr><tr><td class="k">друг</td><td class="k">друзья</td></tr><tr><td class="k">брат</td><td class="k">братья</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Это мои друзья.|EH-tah mah-EE drooz'-YAH|These are my friends.}}
{{ex:Где дети?|gdyeh DYEH-tee|Where are the kids?}}
<h3>Words that agree change too</h3>
<p>In the plural, gender disappears. <b>мой</b>, <b>моя</b> and <b>моё</b> all become <b>мои</b>, and adjectives end in <b>-ые</b>, or <b>-ие</b> after the letters of the spelling rule: <b>новые</b>, but <b>русские</b>, <b>хорошие</b>. <b>Это</b> never changes: <b>Это мои сумки</b>.</p>
{{ex:Это хорошие люди.|EH-tah khah-ROH-shih-yih LYOO-dee|These are good people.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> adding -s, or writing ы after к or г. <b>книгы</b> → <b>книги</b>, <b>сумкы</b> → <b>сумки</b>. Read the Cyrillic first, and use the respelling only to check where the stress falls.</div>
`,
