    body: `
<p>English has two present tenses: "I read" and "I am reading". Russian has one for both, and no word for "am": <b>Я читаю</b> covers both. What changes is the ending of the verb, which shows who does the action. Because the ending already shows the person, Russians often drop <b>я</b> in a short answer: asked <b>Ты работаешь?</b>, you can say just <b>Работаю</b>. With he, she and they, keep the subject.</p>
<h3>First conjugation: the -ешь verbs</h3>
<p>Take the infinitive <b>читать</b> (to read), drop <b>-ть</b> and add these endings. <b>работать</b> (to work), <b>знать</b> (to know), <b>делать</b> (to do) and <b>понимать</b> (to understand) work the same way.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>читать</th></tr><tr><td>I</td><td class="k">я читаю</td></tr><tr><td>you (informal)</td><td class="k">ты читаешь</td></tr><tr><td>he, she</td><td class="k">он, она читает</td></tr><tr><td>we</td><td class="k">мы читаем</td></tr><tr><td>you (formal, or you all)</td><td class="k">вы читаете</td></tr><tr><td>they</td><td class="k">они читают</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Я читаю.|yah chee-TAH-yoo|I read. / I'm reading.}}
{{ex:Что вы делаете?|shtoh vih DYEH-lah-yih-tih|What are you doing? (formal)}}
<h3>Second conjugation: the -ишь verbs</h3>
<p>Verbs like <b>говорить</b> (to speak) drop <b>-ить</b> and take endings with и: <b>говорю, говоришь, говорит, говорим, говорите, говорят</b>. <b>любить</b> (to love, to like a lot) adds an л in the я form only: <b>я люблю</b>, but <b>ты любишь</b>.</p>
{{ex:Вы говорите по-русски?|vih gah-vah-REE-tih pah-ROOS-kee|Do you speak Russian? (formal)}}
{{ex:Я люблю чай.|yah lyoo-BLYOO chai|I love tea.}}
<h3>Two verbs to learn as they are</h3>
<p><b>хотеть</b> (to want) mixes the two patterns: <b>я хочу, ты хочешь, он хочет</b>, but <b>мы хотим, вы хотите, они хотят</b>. <b>жить</b> (to live) adds a в: <b>я живу, ты живёшь, они живут</b>.</p>
{{ex:Мы хотим кофе.|mih khah-TEEM KOH-fih|We want coffee.}}
{{ex:Где ты живёшь?|gdyeh tih zhih-VYOHSH|Where do you live? (informal)}}
<h3>The words around the verb still agree</h3>
<p>A present-tense verb does not change for gender, but the words with the subject do: <b>Мой брат работает</b>, <b>Моя сестра работает</b>, <b>Мои друзья работают</b>.</p>
{{ex:Моя сестра работает.|mah-YAH sihs-TRAH rah-BOH-tah-yiht|My sister works.}}
<h3>ты or вы</h3>
<p>Use the вы form, which ends in <b>-те</b>, with strangers and with anyone older or senior to you. Use the ты form with friends and family. Mixing them up is a real mistake, not a small one: <b>вы говоришь</b> is as wrong as "they speaks". If in doubt, choose вы; nobody minds being treated politely.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using the wrong ending, or adding a word for "am". <b>я работает</b> → <b>я работаю</b>; <b>Я есть читаю</b> → <b>Я читаю</b>. Read the Cyrillic first, and use the respelling only to check where the stress falls.</div>
`,
