    body: `
<p>In this course numbers are written as words, the way you say them aloud. Learn zero to ten first: most bigger numbers are built from them. Watch the stress, which is not where an English speaker expects it in several of them: <b>четыре</b> is stressed in the middle, and <b>одиннадцать</b> on the second syllable. The respelling shows each one.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>0–5</th><th>6–10</th></tr><tr><td class="k">ноль, один, два, три, четыре, пять</td><td class="k">шесть, семь, восемь, девять, десять</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>Bigger numbers</h3>
<p>Eleven to nineteen end in <b>-надцать</b>: <b>одиннадцать, двенадцать, пятнадцать</b>. The tens are <b>двадцать, тридцать, сорок, пятьдесят</b>, the hundreds <b>сто, двести, триста, пятьсот</b>, and a thousand is <b>тысяча</b>. Put the parts side by side, biggest first, with no "and".</p>
{{ex:двадцать пять|DVAH-tsaht' pyaht'|twenty-five}}
{{ex:сто двадцать|stoh DVAH-tsaht'|a hundred twenty}}
<h3>One and two agree</h3>
<p>English numbers never change. In Russian, "one" and "two" agree with the noun's gender. "One" is <b>один</b> with a masculine noun, <b>одна</b> with a feminine one and <b>одно</b> with a neuter one. "Two" is <b>два</b>, but <b>две</b> before a feminine noun.</p>
{{ex:один билет|ah-DEEN bee-LYEHT|one ticket}}
{{ex:одна бутылка|ahd-NAH boo-TIHL-kah|one bottle}}
{{ex:две бутылки воды|dvyeh boo-TIHL-kee vah-DIH|two bottles of water}}
<h3>Rubles: three forms</h3>
<p>English says "one ruble, two rubles, five rubles". Russian has three forms, and the last number you say picks the form:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Last number</th><th>Form</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td class="k">один рубль</td></tr><tr><td>2, 3, 4</td><td class="k">два рубля</td></tr><tr><td>5 to 20</td><td class="k">пять рублей</td></tr></table></div>
<p>So <b>двадцать один рубль</b> and <b>двадцать два рубля</b>, but <b>двадцать пять рублей</b>. Round numbers such as <b>сто</b> and <b>тысяча</b> take <b>рублей</b>. Learn these as fixed phrases for now; the lesson on the genitive explains them.</p>
{{ex:Это стоит двести рублей.|EH-tah STOH-eet DVYEHS-tee roo-BLYEY|It costs two hundred rubles.}}
<h3>Asking the price</h3>
<p><b>Сколько стоит?</b> asks about one thing; <b>Сколько стоят?</b> asks about several. The answer usually leaves out the verb: <b>Сто рублей</b>, "A hundred rubles". Prices in this course are round, made-up numbers, so do not use them to plan a budget.</p>
{{ex:Сколько стоят билеты?|SKOHL'-kah STOH-yaht bee-LYEH-tih|How much are the tickets?}}
<h3>Age and phone numbers</h3>
<p>Age is a fixed phrase: <b>Мне двадцать лет</b> (I'm twenty). Phone numbers are read in pairs: 45 12 is <b>сорок пять, двенадцать</b>.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> one plural for every number. <b>два рубли</b> → <b>два рубля</b>; <b>пять рубля</b> → <b>пять рублей</b>. Read the Cyrillic first, and use the respelling only to check where the stress falls.</div>
`,
