    id: "prepositional",
    title: "Prepositional: where, about",
    blurb: "Say where someone is (в Москве, на работе) and what you think about (о маме) with the prepositional case.",
    deck: "Home and city",
    body: `
<p>English shows a noun's job with word order and small words like "in" and "about". Russian uses small words too, but it also changes the end of the noun. Each set of endings is called a <b>case</b>. The basic form you have used so far, as in <b>Это Москва</b>, is the nominative case. A case is not a new word to learn, just a new ending on a word you know. Russian has six cases; this lesson teaches the one for places and topics, and the next few lessons add the others one at a time.</p>
<h3>Where? в or на + the prepositional case</h3>
<p>To say where something is, use <b>в</b> (in) or <b>на</b> (on, at) and change the ending of the noun. That ending is the prepositional case. For most nouns it is <b>-е</b>, whatever the gender.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Basic form</th><th>Where?</th></tr><tr><td class="k">Москва</td><td class="k">в Москве</td></tr><tr><td class="k">парк</td><td class="k">в парке</td></tr><tr><td class="k">работа</td><td class="k">на работе</td></tr><tr><td class="k">почта</td><td class="k">на почте</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Я живу в Москве.|yah zhih-VOO v mahs-KVYEH|I live in Moscow.}}
{{ex:Мы в парке.|mih f PAHR-kih|We're in the park.}}
<h3>в or на?</h3>
<p><b>в</b> means "inside": <b>в школе</b>, <b>в банке</b>, <b>в Москве</b>. A few places take <b>на</b> instead, and you learn them with the word: <b>на работе</b> (at work), <b>на почте</b> (at the post office), <b>на улице</b> (outside).</p>
{{ex:Она на работе.|ah-NAH nah rah-BOH-tih|She's at work.}}
{{ex:Анна на почте.|AHN-nah nah POHCH-tih|Anna is at the post office.}}
<h3>Two other endings</h3>
<p>Nouns that end in <b>-ия</b> take <b>-ии</b>: <b>Россия → в России</b>. Feminine nouns that end in <b>ь</b> take <b>-и</b>: <b>Казань → в Казани</b>.</p>
{{ex:Мой брат живёт в Казани.|moy braht zhih-VYOHT f kah-ZAH-nee|My brother lives in Kazan.}}
<h3>About: о + the prepositional case</h3>
<p><b>о</b> means "about" and takes the same endings: <b>о маме</b>, <b>о Москве</b>. Before a vowel it becomes <b>об</b>: <b>об Анне</b>.</p>
{{ex:Я думаю о маме.|yah DOO-mah-yoo ah MAH-mih|I'm thinking about Mom.}}
<h3>Adjectives change too</h3>
<p>An adjective in front of the noun gets its own ending: <b>-ом</b> for masculine and neuter, <b>-ой</b> for feminine: <b>в новом доме</b>, <b>в новой школе</b>. For now, just recognize these. Answers to <b>Где?</b> (where?) come in this case. A good habit: whenever you say where someone is, check the ending of the place before you say it. English speakers get the preposition right and forget the ending.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> keeping the basic form after в. <b>Я живу в Москва</b> → <b>Я живу в Москве</b>. Read the Cyrillic first, and use the respelling only to check where the stress falls.</div>
`,
