    deck: "Shopping and things",
    body: `
<p>English uses "of": a bottle of water, the center of town. Russian has no word for "of". It puts the second noun after the first and changes its ending. That ending is the genitive case. The order is the reverse of an English possessive: "Moscow's center" is <b>центр Москвы</b>, the center first and then the owner. Think "center of Moscow" and the Russian order follows.</p>
<h3>The endings</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Noun</th><th>Ending</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>masculine, neuter</td><td>add -а (-я after й or ь)</td><td class="k">хлеб → хлеба, чай → чая, молоко → молока</td></tr><tr><td>feminine in -а</td><td>-а → -ы (-и after the spelling-rule letters)</td><td class="k">вода → воды, сумка → сумки</td></tr><tr><td>feminine in -я</td><td>-я → -и</td><td class="k">кухня → кухни</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:бутылка воды|boo-TIHL-kah vah-DIH|a bottle of water}}
{{ex:чашка чая|CHAHSH-kah CHAH-yah|a cup of tea}}
{{ex:центр города|tsehntr GOH-rah-dah|downtown (the center of town)}}
<h3>нет and без</h3>
<p>"There is no…" is <b>нет</b> + the genitive case. "Without" is <b>без</b> + the genitive case. Now you can see why it was <b>Здесь нет банка</b>.</p>
{{ex:Нет хлеба.|nyeht KHLYEH-bah|There's no bread.}}
{{ex:Кофе без сахара, пожалуйста.|KOH-fih bihs SAH-khah-rah pah-ZHAHL-stah|Coffee without sugar, please.}}
<h3>How much</h3>
<p><b>сколько</b> (how much), <b>много</b> (a lot of) and <b>мало</b> (not much) take the genitive case too: <b>Сколько сахара?</b>, <b>много молока</b>. For things you count, they take the genitive plural, which has its own endings; for now, learn <b>Сколько стоит?</b> and use these words with things like milk, water and sugar.</p>
<h3>Numbers explained</h3>
<p>The ruble forms make sense now. After 2, 3 and 4, a noun takes the genitive singular: <b>два рубля</b>, <b>три часа</b>. From 5 to 20 it takes the genitive plural: <b>пять рублей</b>, <b>пять часов</b>. After 1 it keeps the basic form: <b>один рубль</b>, <b>один час</b>.</p>
{{ex:три часа|tree chih-SAH|three hours}}
<h3>Small words that take it</h3>
<p><b>из</b> (from a place), <b>у</b> (at someone's), <b>после</b> (after) and <b>для</b> (for) all take the genitive case: <b>после урока</b>, <b>для мамы</b>. You will meet many more as you go. When you learn a new small word like these, learn which case comes after it at the same time.</p>
{{ex:Я из Бостона.|yah eez BOHS-tah-nah|I'm from Boston.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> keeping the basic form, or using English "of" order. <b>нет хлеб</b> → <b>нет хлеба</b>; <b>воды бутылка</b> → <b>бутылка воды</b>. Read the Cyrillic first, and use the respelling only to check where the stress falls.</div>
`,
